Parallel execution of independent ready units is required by default. Execute only coupled steps in the main session. Before starting any implementation, inventory units and dependencies, then dispatch every ready unit concurrently in one wave to sub-agents. The main session owns shared contracts, integration, cross-unit verification, and artifact submission; workers own only their declared files. Plans without declared units but with independent ready steps (real dependencies, exact authorized ownership) are treated as the same ready group and dispatched too. When the plan declares ready units, dispatch ready units concurrently; when it does not, the unit-less dispatch rule above already covers that case. Respect dependencies, collect each unit's implementation and verification proof, then integrate and run cross-unit and full verification in the main session. Never let two agents edit the same file; keep integration, final verification, and artifact submission in the main session.

Keep dispatch slots full: when a unit finishes, validate its proof, release its dependents, and dispatch them immediately. Recompute readiness after each result; list order alone is not a dependency. Dispatching is always subject to the worker cap, ownership rules, and wave queuing below — required-by-default never overrides those safety rules.

Sequential execution requires an explicit plan reason or a missing sub-agent tool. It is permitted only when (a) the plan explicitly declares the work sequential or coupled (a wholly linear plan that explains its coupling, or `Depends on:` chains), or (b) the runtime exposes no sub-agent / task tool for this attempt (use the bounded-sequential fallback below). A plan that looks too ambitious to finish in one session is `not a reason to stop, hand back, split the task, or return `partial`` — that is the cue to fan out further, not to stop. If the developer executes sequentially while the plan declares two or more independent ready units, the development result must state `which plan text or runtime limit forced it`; "it was simpler" is not a valid reason.

Use only the orchestration tools actually exposed by the running runtime. A full dispatch slot means a queued wave plus useful local work on the next safe increment, never idle abandonment. When no native sub-agent / task tool is exposed by the runtime — or the available one is unavailable for this attempt — fall back to bounded sequential progress on the same dependency graph: pick the next ready reference, implement it, verify it, and continue; do not invent tool calls or produce a size-only refusal. When the runtime cannot dispatch declared units (cap exhausted, ownership unresolvable, malformed graph), keep that work in the main session rather than broadening scope.

Ownership may come from a unit's exact `Paths:`, its `Directories:` (the declared directory limits), or its steps' `Files:` when the unit has neither. Do not broaden file ownership to a parent directory. Remove assignments to `.agent`, `.git`, and `.worktrees` (and descendants) before making a worker brief. An unknown or empty effective scope, malformed dependency graph, or unextractable ownership stays in the main session; it never grants broad worker writes.

A worker cap limits concurrent units, not total units: queue later ready units in waves. Serialize conflicting ownership—equal files, directory ancestry, and directory/file containment conflict—until the earlier writer finishes. Units with disjoint file ownership in one directory may proceed together. Recompute readiness after each result; list order alone is not a dependency.

Before transferring ownership from one writer to another (a new worker, a fallback, or a re-dispatch), confirm the former writer has actually stopped — the assignment is closed, the result is persisted, and no new work is in flight on the same path. Never overlap writers on the same file. Release a dependent step only after the upstream writer's evidence is validated, not on a self-declared completion.

For every dispatched unit, give the worker its unit ID, assigned steps, exact effective ownership, focused verification, and the brokered-tools-only rule. Workers do not commit, integrate, or submit the main artifact. Their result is a lead the main session checks before final reconciliation. Raw plan prose remains authoritative: retain all residual prose work and unowned global steps in the main session, even when extraction cannot represent them as worker assignments.
